  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as wire-format substrate evidence, but essentially nonresponsive to this routing-policy scenario: it explores envelope encoding, unknown-pCID behavior, and signature placement, not policy-relative route selection or audit of conflicting peer preferences.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear envelope/payload separation keeps application policy out of the carrier layer.",
      "Variant-matrix, non-canonical lineage is relatively safe for future evolution.",
      "Unknown-pCID and signature probes expose some interoperability failure modes."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No model of route advertisements, route classes, or path ranking.",
      "No peer-specific preference/refusal semantics for Alice versus Carol.",
      "No local promise-accounting record for why one route was chosen over another."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Could be mistaken for a routing-policy solution when it only defines message carriage.",
      "Open question about whether pCID carries routing metadata risks layer leakage.",
      "Different unknown-pCID behaviors could fragment routing-message interoperability."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "How should routing-policy payloads be represented inside a pCID-defined envelope?",
      "What evidence should Bob record so Alice and Carol can locally audit policy-relative choices?",
      "Should routing-policy messages tolerate unknown pCIDs or fail closed?"
    ],
